Yes, you can determine if a Slack message has been read or not using the Slack API. When you retrieve a message by its ID, the Slack API provides a `read` property in the message object that indicates whether the message has been read or not.

Here's an example of how to retrieve a message and check its read status using the Slack API:

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');

async function getMessage(messageId) {
  try {
    const response = await axios.get(`https://slack.com/api/messages.read`, {
      params: {
        token: 'YOUR_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN',
        channel: 'CHANNEL_ID',
        message: messageId,
      },
    });

    const message = response.data.messages[0];
    console.log(`Message ID: ${message.id}`);
    console.log(`Read status: ${message.read}`);

    return message;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    return null;
  }
}

getMessage('YOUR_MESSAGE_ID')
  .then((message) => {
    if (message) {
      console.log(`Message read status: ${message.read}`);
    } else {
      console.log('Error fetching message.');
    }
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error('Error fetching message:', error);
  });
```

Replace `YOUR_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN` with your bot's token, and `CHANNEL_ID` with the channel where the message was sent.

The `read` property will be set to `true` if the message has been read, and `false` if it hasn't. Keep in mind that the read status is determined by the Slack client, so it might not be immediately updated after a message is read.

To set up a Docker Compose server with Nginx, Let's Encrypt Certbot, PHP 8.1, and MariaDB, you will need the following files and configurations:

1. `docker-compose.yml` file: This file will define the services, networks, and volumes for your application.

```yaml
version: '3.8'
services:
  web:
    image: nginx:latest
    ports:
      - "80:80"
      - "443:443"
    volumes:
      - ./nginx/conf.d:/etc/nginx/conf.d
      - ./nginx/html:/usr/share/nginx/html
    depends_on:
      - php
      - mariadb
  php:
    image: php:8.1-fpm
    volumes:
      - ./php:/var/www/html
    depends_on:
      - mariadb
  mariadb:
    image: mariadb:latest
    environment:
      - MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=password
      - MYSQL_DATABASE=mydatabase
      - MYSQL_USER=myusername
      - MYSQL_PASSWORD=mypassword
    volumes:
      - ./mariadb:/var/lib/mysql
```

2. `nginx/conf.d/default.conf` file: This file will contain the Nginx configuration for your application.

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;

    location / {
        root /usr/share/nginx/html;
        index index.php index.html index.htm;
    }

    location ~ \.php$ {
        fastcgi_pass php:9000;
        fastcgi_index index.php;
        include fastcgi_params;
    }
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name example.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem;

